<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Continuity as a hypothesis: reading for after the epsilon-delta proofs day.  -->
<!-- Mirrors Section 0.5: each proof applies the epsilon-delta property to a       -->
<!-- positive number it chooses (the box in class materials).                      -->
<!-- Parsons problems: one sentence per block.                                     -->
<section xml:id="sec-continuity_hypothesis" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Continuity as a Hypothesis</title>

  <p>
    In <xref ref="sec-convergence_hypothesis"/>, a convergence statement was something we <em>knew</em>, and each proof applied it to a positive number of its own choosing. Continuity works the same way. If <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m>, then the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property (<xref ref="thm-epsilon-delta"/>) holds for <em>every</em> positive number, so a proof may apply it to whichever positive number it needs: <m>\epsilon</m>, <m>\epsilon/2</m>, or a fixed number such as <m>1</m> or <m>f(a)</m>. The sentence has the chosen number in both places:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Because <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m> and <m>\square\gt 0</m>, by the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property there exists <m>\delta\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|f(x)-f(a)|\lt\square</m>.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-sign-preservation">
    <title>Continuous functions keep their sign nearby</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m> and <m>f(a)\gt 0</m>. Then there exists <m>\delta\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>f(x)\gt 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Assume <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m> and <m>f(a)\gt 0</m>. We must prove that there exists <m>\delta\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>f(x)\gt 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m> and <m>f(a)\gt 0</m>, by the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property there exists <m>\delta\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|f(x)-f(a)|\lt f(a)</m>. With this <m>\delta</m>, we will show that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>f(x)\gt 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>. Then <m>|f(x)-f(a)|\lt f(a)</m>, so <m>-f(a)\lt f(x)-f(a)</m>. Adding <m>f(a)</m> to both sides gives <m>f(x)\gt 0</m>, as desired.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <remark>
    <p>
      The goal has no <m>\epsilon</m> in it, so the proof never says <q>Let <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>.</q> The number in the box is <m>f(a)</m>, the distance from <m>f(a)</m> down to <m>0</m>. This is the function version of the third problem in <xref ref="sec-convergence_hypothesis"/>, where <m>L-a</m> went in the box. A proof with the sequential definition is possible too, but it needs a proof by contradiction.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercises xml:id="parsons-continuity-hypothesis">
    <title>Proof practice: using continuity</title>

    <!-- box: the fixed number 1 (compare Theorem 9.1) -->
    <exercise label="parsons-locally-bounded" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
      <title>Continuous functions are bounded nearby</title>
      <statement><div>Suppose <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m>. Put the given blocks in a correct order to prove that there exist <m>\delta\gt 0</m> and <m>M\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|f(x)|\le M</m>. Some blocks are not needed.</div></statement>
      <blocks>
        <block name="1" order="6">
          <div>Assume <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="2" depends="1" order="3">
          <div>We must prove that there exist <m>\delta\gt 0</m> and <m>M\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|f(x)|\le M</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="3" depends="2" order="9">
          <div>Because <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m> and <m>1\gt 0</m>, by the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property there exists <m>\delta\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|f(x)-f(a)|\lt 1</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="4" depends="2" order="1">
          <div>Choose <m>M=|f(a)|+1</m>, which is positive.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="5" depends="3 4" order="7">
          <div>With this <m>\delta</m> and this <m>M</m>, we will show that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|f(x)|\le M</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="6" depends="5" order="4">
          <div>Let <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="7" depends="6" order="8">
          <div>By the triangle inequality, <m>|f(x)|\le|f(x)-f(a)|+|f(a)|\lt 1+|f(a)|=M</m>, as desired.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="8" correct="no" order="2">
          <div>Let <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="9" correct="no" order="5">
          <div>Choose <m>M=1</m>.</div>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <!-- two hypotheses: delta = min{delta_1, delta_2} (compare the squeeze lemma 8.5(a)) -->
    <exercise label="parsons-squeeze-continuous" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
      <title>A squeeze lemma for continuous functions</title>
      <statement><div>Let <m>f</m>, <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> be functions with domain <m>\R</m>. Suppose that for all <m>x\in\R</m>, <m>g(x)\le f(x)\le h(x)</m>, that <m>g(a)=h(a)</m>, and that <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> are continuous at <m>a</m>. Put the given blocks in a correct order to prove that <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m>. Some blocks are not needed.</div></statement>
      <blocks>
        <block name="1" order="9">
          <div>Assume that for all <m>x\in\R</m>, <m>g(x)\le f(x)\le h(x)</m>, that <m>g(a)=h(a)</m>, and that <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> are continuous at <m>a</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="2" depends="1" order="4">
          <div>Since <m>g(a)\le f(a)\le h(a)</m> and <m>g(a)=h(a)</m>, we have <m>f(a)=g(a)=h(a)</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="3" depends="2" order="12">
          <div>By the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property, we must prove that for all <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>, there exists <m>\delta\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\R</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|f(x)-f(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="4" depends="3" order="1">
          <div>Let <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="5" depends="4" order="14">
          <div>Because <m>g</m> is continuous at <m>a</m> and <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>, by the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property there exists <m>\delta_1\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\R</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta_1</m>, <m>|g(x)-g(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="6" depends="4" order="6">
          <div>Because <m>h</m> is continuous at <m>a</m> and <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>, by the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property there exists <m>\delta_2\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\R</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta_2</m>, <m>|h(x)-h(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="7" depends="5 6" order="10">
          <div>Choose <m>\delta=\min\{\delta_1,\delta_2\}</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="8" depends="7" order="2">
          <div>With this <m>\delta</m>, we will show that for all <m>x\in\R</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|f(x)-f(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="9" depends="8" order="13">
          <div>Let <m>x\in\R</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="10" depends="9" order="7">
          <div>Then <m>|x-a|\lt\delta_1</m> and <m>|x-a|\lt\delta_2</m>, so <m>|g(x)-g(a)|\lt\epsilon</m> and <m>|h(x)-h(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="11" depends="10" order="3">
          <div>Since <m>f(a)=h(a)</m> and <m>f(x)\le h(x)</m>, we get <m>f(x)-f(a)\le h(x)-h(a)\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="12" depends="10" order="11">
          <div>Since <m>f(a)=g(a)</m> and <m>f(x)\ge g(x)</m>, we get <m>f(x)-f(a)\ge g(x)-g(a)\gt -\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="13" depends="11 12" order="5">
          <div>Thus <m>|f(x)-f(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>, as desired.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="14" correct="no" order="8">
          <div>Choose <m>\delta=\max\{\delta_1,\delta_2\}</m>.</div>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <!-- half of Ross Theorem 17.2: the convergence hypothesis is applied to delta -->
    <exercise label="parsons-epsdelta-to-sequences" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
      <title>From the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property to sequences</title>
      <statement><div>Suppose <m>f</m> has the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property at <m>a</m>. Put the given blocks in a correct order to prove that for every sequence <m>(x_n)</m> in <m>\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> such that <m>x_n\to a</m>, we have <m>f(x_n)\to f(a)</m>. Some blocks are not needed.</div></statement>
      <blocks>
        <block name="1" order="7">
          <div>Assume that for all <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>, there exists <m>\delta\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|f(x)-f(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="2" depends="1" order="3">
          <div>Let <m>(x_n)</m> be a sequence in <m>\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> such that <m>x_n\to a</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="3" depends="2" order="10">
          <div>We must prove that <m>f(x_n)\to f(a)</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="4" depends="3" order="1">
          <div>Let <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="5" depends="4" order="9">
          <div>By our assumption, there exists <m>\delta\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|f(x)-f(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="6" depends="5" order="5">
          <div>Because <m>x_n\to a</m> and <m>\delta\gt 0</m>, by definition there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|x_n-a|\lt\delta</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="7" depends="6" order="11">
          <div>With this <m>N</m>, we will show that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|f(x_n)-f(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="8" depends="7" order="2">
          <div>Let <m>n\in\N</m> with <m>n\gt N</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="9" depends="8" order="6">
          <div>Then <m>x_n\in\operatorname{dom}(f)</m> and <m>|x_n-a|\lt\delta</m>, so <m>|f(x_n)-f(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="10" depends="9" order="4">
          <div>Thus for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|f(x_n)-f(a)|\lt\epsilon</m>, so by definition <m>f(x_n)\to f(a)</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="11" correct="no" order="8">
          <div>Because <m>x_n\to a</m> and <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>, by definition there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|x_n-a|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-constant-multiple-continuous">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>c\in\R</m>, and suppose <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m>. Use the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property to prove that the function <m>cf</m> is continuous at <m>a</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Apply the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property for <m>f</m> to <m>\epsilon/(|c|+1)</m>, which is positive even when <m>c=0</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-order-preserved">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be functions with domain <m>\R</m> that are continuous at <m>a</m>, and suppose <m>f(a)\lt g(a)</m>. Prove that there exists <m>\delta\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>x\in\R</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>f(x)\lt g(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Apply the <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> property to both <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> with <m>(g(a)-f(a))/2</m> in the box, and take the smaller of the two <m>\delta</m>'s.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
